Detailed Behavioral Case Evidence

Case 1: AK (24, Junior QA, Bengaluru) — The Salary Mismatch Penalty

- Investment: ₹1,500/month in Nifty 50 Index Fund.
- The Incident: Set debit date to the 5th. In Month 4, his company's payroll
    was delayed to the 7th due to a bank holiday weekend. On the 5th, his
    account held ₹380. HDFC deducted an ECS return fee of ₹590.
- User Action: "I lost ₹590 because of a ₹1,500 investment. It felt like I was
    being penalized for saving money. I immediately paused the SIP on Groww so
    it wouldn't happen again. It's been 3 months and I haven't resumed."
- Root Insight: A single bank penalty can offset months of nominal portfolio
    growth, turning an automated habit into a perceived financial liability.

Case 2: SM (26, Content Ops, Pune) — The Micro-Corpus Perception Gap

- Investment: ₹1,000/month across 5 months (Total: ₹5,000).
- The Incident: Checked her portfolio in Month 5. Total value was ₹5,042 (a
    nominal gain of ₹42).
- User Action: "I was disciplined for 5 months. But ₹42 profit after half a
    year felt completely unrewarding. My savings account gives more than that
    without any stress. When an expense came up for a family wedding, I stopped
    the SIP."
- Root Insight: Showing small nominal gains on early-stage portfolios fails to
    reinforce the habit. Without contextual framing, the perceived value of
    continuing remains low.

Case 3: RV (23, BPO Executive, Delhi) — Discretionary Liquidation

- Investment: ₹1,000/month in Flexi Cap Fund.
- The Incident: In Month 6, his two-wheeler required an urgent clutch repair
    costing ₹3,800. His Groww portfolio held ~₹6,100.
- User Action: "Groww never showed me what my money was working toward. It
    just looked like cash sitting in an account. I withdrew the balance for my
    bike and cancelled the SIP."
- Root Insight: If micro-investments are not linked to an explicit purpose or
    milestone, they are treated as spare cash reserves to be liquidated for
    short-term expenses.

Case 4: MP (28, Government Teacher, Bhopal) — The Retained Outlier

- Investment: ₹2,500/month in Large & Mid Cap Fund. Active for 9 months.
- Why She Stayed: Her government salary credits on the 1st without fail. Her
    father advised her: "Treat this like your Provident Fund deduction. Don't
    look at the app."
- Root Insight: Habit persistence often depends on regular, predictable cash
    flows and low dashboard engagement during early compounding stages.

Case 5: TD (25, Freelance Designer, Jaipur) — The Variable Income Dilemma

- Investment: ₹2,000/month in Small Cap Fund.
- The Incident: Income fluctuates between ₹30,000 and ₹60,000. Client invoice
    payments were delayed into the third week of the month.
- User Action: "I didn't want to cancel my investment. I just wanted to shift
    this month's payment to the 22nd. But Groww required a 3-day buffer and
    changing the date felt complicated. I had to pause it instead, and I forgot
    to restart."
- Root Insight: Rigid calendar deductions clash directly with variable income
    patterns, turning temporary liquidity crunches into permanent churn.

Case 6: DR (27, Pharmacist, Mumbai) — The Dashboard Panic Survivor

- Investment: ₹2,000/month. Active for 8 months.
- The Incident: During a market correction, his ₹16,000 portfolio dropped by
    ₹720 in one day. The Groww home screen highlighted the negative 1-day return
    in red, prompting him to consider stopping.
- User Action: He asked an accountant friend who advised him to ignore daily
    market swings. He retained the SIP, but noted: "If I hadn't asked someone
    with finance experience, I would have stopped it right then to preserve my
    capital."
- Root Insight: Novice investors often interpret short-term market
    fluctuations through a trading lens, increasing anxiety in the absence of
    reassuring context.

